#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:9
msgid "Why magi_depyf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:11
msgid ""
"`magi_depyf` is the compilation observability layer for `MagiCompiler`. "
"It answers three practical questions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:14
msgid "**What exactly happened during compilation?**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:15
msgid ""
"**Did the outcome match expectations?** (for example: graph split shape, "
"cache reuse, retry behavior)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:16
msgid "**If something failed, where should I look first?**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:20
msgid "1. Positioning: Built-in observability for MagiCompiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:22
msgid ""
"In a real MagiCompiler pipeline, compilation spans multiple stages: "
"Dynamo capture, Magi backend graph transforms/splitting, Inductor "
"codegen, and AOT/JIT reuse. Plain logs are often not enough to reliably "
"answer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:25
msgid "Did the failure happen at full-graph level or in a specific subgraph?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:26
msgid "Did a pass change graph behavior unexpectedly?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:27
msgid "Why did this run miss cache?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:29
msgid ""
"`magi_depyf` writes these signals as structured events and artifacts on "
"disk, so you can replay, compare, and debug deterministically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:33
msgid "2. Primary scenario: `magi_compile`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:35
msgid "2.1 Recommended usage model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:37
msgid ""
"For `magi_compile` users, `magi_depyf` is most useful as a built-in "
"observability layer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:39
msgid "Streams events to `timeline_events/timeline.jsonl` during compilation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:40
msgid "Writes event artifacts under `timeline_events/files/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:41
msgid "Exports a structured `compiled_functions/` artifact tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:43
msgid ""
"In most cases, you do not need to manually wire custom hooks. Run through"
" the MagiCompiler path and inspect the output directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:45
msgid "2.2 What you get automatically in the Magi compile path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:47
msgid ""
"For the `magi_compile` scenario, you do **not** need to call "
"`explain_compilation` manually. As long as the model runs through "
"MagiCompiler, `magi_depyf` outputs are written automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:50
msgid "For example, one real run produced:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:52
msgid "`magi_depyf_torch_compile_debug/magi_depyf/run_20260322_192147/model_1_TwoLayerTransformer_rank_0/timeline_events`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:54
msgid "In general, the output pattern is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:56
msgid "`<cache_root_dir>/magi_depyf/run_*/model_*_rank_*/timeline_events/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:58
msgid "Key artifacts under this directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:60
msgid "`timeline.jsonl`: streaming event log written during compilation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:61
msgid "`files/0000_*`, `files/0001_*`, ... event folders with attached artifacts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:62
msgid "`files/*/attributes.json`: structured metadata for each event folder"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:64
msgid "Related compiled artifact view is also emitted under:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/blog/why_magi_depyf.md:66
msgid "`<cache_root_dir>/magi_depyf/run_*/model_*_rank_*/compiled_functions/`"
msgstr ""

